(() => {
  "use strict";

  const searchIndex = [{"title":"Overview","route":"/","group":"Start","summary":"The independent kernel, familiar desktop, current progress and main visitor routes.","text":"Baryon OS Rust kernel familiar desktop app permissions roadmap QEMU research software"},{"title":"Baryon OS Docs","route":"/docs/","group":"Start","summary":"Browse every visitor guide by suitability, experience, system internals, evidence or project work.","text":"documentation topics guide suitability experience architecture evidence development"},{"title":"Status","route":"/status/","group":"Start","summary":"Accepted roadmap progress, current boundaries and release readiness.","text":"Is Baryon OS ready to install? No. Baryon OS is active research and development software. There is no supported production release, personal-computer installer or accepted daily-use hardware profile. The documented execution route is an isolated QEMU virtual machine. Accepted roadmap progress Open the source-bound status ↗ The fixed roadmap awards points only when a complete criterion has the evidence it requires. It is not a schedule, security percentage or prediction of release time. What has been demonstrated? Independent foundation A project loader and Rust kernel, with native process, capability and service work. Native QEMU paths Selected storage, networking, UI, SDK and recovery behaviors have source-bound guest evidence. Product direction A familiar desktop, Material data model, explicit app authority and connected local-first experience. Research tracks Bounded work on local AI, post-quantum protection and local quantum execution. These statements describe separate accepted or implemented paths. They do not mean that all components form one complete, supported system image. What remains open before daily use? Open work includes broader physical hardware support, accessible end-to-end desktop tasks, everyday applications, native app installation, encrypted multi-device storage, local AI acceptance, full release recovery, endurance, user evaluation and a maintainable distribution path. The binding release matrix retains normal and failure scenarios for each product task. How should a status claim be checked? Use the fixed criteria for formal acceptance, the project-state record for current integration and the verification records for source-bound evidence. A green build, prototype screenshot or host-side test answers a narrower question than a native QEMU run, physical-hardware trial or human evaluation. How the roadmap works → Testing and evidence → Current project state ↗ Release acceptance matrix ↗ Choose your next step Read the suitability guide before deciding whether to follow, test or contribute. Is it for me? → Try in QEMU →"},{"title":"Contribute","route":"/contribute/","group":"Start","summary":"Ways to help through feedback, reports, documentation, testing or code.","text":"Choose the kind of contribution Baryon OS welcomes bounded, evidence-oriented contributions. A useful contribution can improve a confusing explanation, describe a reproducible defect, test an accessibility assumption or implement one agreed system step. Ask, discuss or improve the product direction Use GitHub Discussions for questions, product ideas and accessibility feedback. Describe the task, the confusing behavior and the outcome that would help. Search for an existing topic first so related evidence stays together. Report a defect or documentation problem Use the issue forms for a reproducible defect, documentation correction or bounded implementation proposal. Include the exact source version, environment, steps, observed result and expected result. Do not present host or simulated behavior as native OS evidence. Propose code Read the contribution guide, current project state and the contracts for the subsystem before editing. Keep one owner per write set, preserve unrelated work and state which checks ran. Pull requests remain drafts until their source-bound checks and required reviews are complete. Start with a small interface. The project is broad, but a reviewable change should have one goal, one invariant, an exact write set, acceptance commands and an explicit evidence limit. Report a security problem privately Send exploitable details through GitHub Private Vulnerability Reporting . Do not place vulnerability details, secrets or real user data in a public issue, discussion, pull request or test log. Check licensing before reuse Project-owned software listed in the file register is offered under MPL-2.0. Documentation, third-party components and Baryon OS names and marks are not automatically covered by that software license. Development workflow → Repository guide → Testing and evidence → Contribution rules ↗ Licensing details ↗ Start here Use public channels for normal questions and private reporting for exploitable security details. Open Discussions ↗ Open an issue ↗"},{"title":"About Baryon OS","route":"/docs/project/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"Why the project exists, what the name means and what makes the OS independent.","text":"What is Baryon OS? Baryon OS is experimental research into an independent operating system. The project develops its own Rust kernel, system services and desktop experience, beginning with x86_64, UEFI and QEMU. It has no supported production release. Why “Baryon”? Baryons are composite particles; protons and neutrons are examples. Their composition is a metaphor for a small kernel, separate services and a distinct user environment forming one system. The name does not imply proven safety, stability or a particular hardware capability. Why build another operating system? The design goal is a privacy-respecting European alternative with familiar desktop interactions and useful connected functions. A person should be able to find work, understand where data is stored and see which app can access it. This is a product ambition, not a measured comparison with existing systems. What makes it independent? The native boot chain and kernel are developed by the project rather than built on Linux, BSD, XNU, Windows or Android. Independence does not mean that every compiler, firmware interface, emulator or reviewed library is made by the project. A development host and the guest OS have different roles. Continue with the mission and vision , explore the system architecture , the desktop direction or the current evidence . Name and project overview ↗ Design ambition ↗ Dependency provenance ↗"},{"title":"Mission and vision","route":"/docs/mission/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"The European research question, the mission it produced and the future experience being pursued.","text":"The question that sharpened the project European preference for greater digital autonomy does not automatically produce operating-system migration. In the 2026 Digital Decade Eurobarometer, 58% of respondents agreed that they would be willing to switch to an EU-based digital service provider , even at slightly higher cost. The survey did not ask whether they would install a European operating system. The resulting research question: if European control, privacy and security are valued, why does that preference not translate into mass operating-system switching—and what would a European OS have to offer before people could change without giving up speed, reliability, familiar work or a polished future-facing experience? The reviewed evidence points to more than origin or privacy: necessary applications, hardware, data migration, learned habits, collaboration, support and long-term maintenance all affect switching. Baryon OS therefore treats a privacy claim or independent kernel as a foundation, not as a complete reason to adopt the product. Mission Develop and test an independent European-oriented operating system in which people can work normally, understand where their data goes and control what apps may do—without accepting an outdated, disconnected or technically diminished experience. Vision A person should be able to move to Baryon OS through a safe, understandable route and find a fast, refined and familiar desktop. Everyday apps, hardware, internet access, recovery and maintenance should work as one coherent product. Material should keep work findable and portable between replaceable tools. Personal storage should remain local or explicitly encrypted before it leaves a device. Optional AI should assist on-device within selected context. What makes this a research programme? Each part of the vision is treated as a question that needs evidence. Market and scientific studies inform hypotheses about switching, information organisation, privacy decisions, visual first impressions, notification load, accessibility and human–AI interaction. Technical contracts translate those hypotheses into testable boundaries. Native QEMU, physical hardware and user studies remain distinct evidence levels. What the mission does not claim The 58% result is not an OS adoption forecast. European origin does not prove privacy, security or quality. The project has not demonstrated mass demand, parity with established platforms or a supported daily-use release. Those outcomes require functional compatibility, sustained voluntary use and completed product acceptance. European adoption research ↗ Official Eurobarometer publication ↗ Market and adoption research ↗ Design ambition ↗"},{"title":"Is Baryon OS for me?","route":"/docs/fit/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"A direct suitability guide for curious visitors, testers, contributors and daily-use seekers.","text":"Who is the project useful for today? Baryon OS is useful today for people who want to follow or contribute to operating-system research, inspect an independently developed Rust kernel, or run bounded experiments in QEMU. The repository documents design decisions, source-bound evidence and open limitations in public. Who should wait? Anyone looking for a daily-use desktop, a supported installer, guaranteed application compatibility or protection for sensitive production data should wait. There is no supported release, physical support matrix, maintenance commitment or complete end-to-end product acceptance. A visible desktop or passing subsystem test does not change that boundary. What is the intended future user experience? The product goal is a familiar, polished desktop with local accounts, understandable app permissions, reliable storage, normal internet access and optional encrypted synchronization to explicitly paired storage. Material keeps documents and their access rights independent from any one app. Local AI is intended to work on-device. These are product requirements under active development, not promises that every function already works. How can a decision be made responsibly? Read Status for the current readiness boundary. Review planned capabilities for the intended product scope. Check hardware and release readiness for what is not supported. If the research scope still fits, use the isolated QEMU route or choose a contribution path . Project introduction ↗ Full product acceptance ↗"},{"title":"Planned capabilities","route":"/docs/capabilities/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"The intended product experience, from everyday apps to storage, networking and recovery.","text":"What should ordinary use eventually include? The full product assignment covers first start, sign-in, a familiar desktop, settings, search, windows, documents, recovery and accessibility. It also requires useful applications for browsing, text, images, PDF, media, calculations, screenshots, diagnostics and productivity tasks. The aim is a coherent product rather than a collection of technical demonstrations. How should apps and data work together? Applications are intended to be replaceable tools. Opening one document should grant access to that selected material, not to every file. Installation, provenance, network access, personal-data access and administrative actions are separate decisions. Supported formats, failure states and import/export behavior must be tested before compatibility is claimed. What connected functions are planned? Internet access, name resolution, authenticated transport and per-app destination permissions are product requirements. Optional storage can use an explicitly paired personal or family NAS, or a deliberately selected external provider that receives opaque client-encrypted objects rather than content keys. Authorized devices should resume synchronization while preserving conflict and recovery behavior. What reliability and maintenance are required? A daily-use release requires safe installation, system and app updates, rollback, accessible recovery, repeated cold starts, extended combined workloads, supported hardware, reproducible release artifacts and a maintenance policy. The release matrix includes normal paths and failures; individual accepted subsystems do not substitute for this integrated evidence. Binding acceptance matrix ↗ Apps and permissions → Storage, sync and NAS → Profiles and accessibility →"},{"title":"Why these choices?","route":"/docs/choices/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"The main product and technology decisions explained in everyday language and linked to their evidence.","text":"Why keep the desktop familiar? Changing operating systems already asks people to move files, learn differences and check whether their tools still work. Familiar setup, windows, taskbar, search and shortcuts reduce avoidable learning. Baryon OS adds its own visual identity and new capabilities where they provide value instead of renaming every ordinary action. Why build an independent kernel? The kernel is where memory, processes and access to shared resources are controlled. Developing that foundation inside the project makes the authority model part of Baryon OS rather than a theme placed on another desktop. This is technically important, but it is not enough on its own: users still need apps, drivers, recovery and support. Why organize work around Material? People often care about the document, photograph or project—not which app currently owns it. Material keeps content, revisions and permissions conceptually separate from tools. Familiar folders, names and search remain available because research shows that personal organization differs and learned methods still matter. Why are app permissions narrow? Opening one document should not expose every file. Installing an app should not silently grant network, device or administrator access. Separate, understandable permissions make it possible to replace a tool, revoke one task or choose a different storage location without surrendering unrelated data. Why is local-first still connected? Privacy should not mean an isolated or inconvenient computer. Local work remains possible, while normal internet access and explicitly paired storage are product requirements. Remote providers may store client-encrypted objects only after a deliberate choice; they do not become silent fallbacks or content-key holders. Why is accessibility part of the identity? People differ in vision, motion sensitivity, reading, attention, hearing and input. One rigid “accessible mode” cannot represent all of those needs. The design uses a usable baseline plus combinable preferences for contrast, motion, typography, stimulus and input, while essential information and safety remain present in every theme. Why is AI optional and on-device? An assistant can help with selected material, but it should not require uploading personal context or make ordinary work depend on a model. Inference is therefore required to stay on the device, with separate model downloads, limited context and revocable actions. Suggestions must remain reviewable and correctable. Why show evidence and unfinished work so explicitly? A polished prototype can create the impression that a whole OS works. The project therefore labels design, host prototype, native QEMU, physical hardware and human evaluation separately. This lets visitors see both the ambition and the actual boundary without turning research into a product claim. No single study proves that these choices will make people switch. Together they form research-backed hypotheses and binding design requirements that still need technical acceptance and trials with real users. Research index ↗ Consumer and adoption evidence ↗ Desktop and Material → Privacy and data control → Testing and evidence →"},{"title":"Roadmap and evidence","route":"/docs/progress/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"How development progress is measured and how to interpret the available evidence.","text":"What does the roadmap measure? The public roadmap has 100 fixed, weighted acceptance points. A criterion earns its points only when it is fully accepted with the evidence it requires. Partial implementation receives no partial points. The score is not elapsed time, a security rating or a release forecast. Development progress Read the current score on GitHub ↗ What exists today? The repository contains a native boot chain, kernel, process and capability infrastructure, services, drivers, an SDK and native UI work. Integration and acceptance differ across components. The current project record identifies what was implemented, what was tested and what remains open; the existence of a directory or a passing build does not establish a complete OS. What evidence counts? Host tests check selected code on a development OS. QEMU tests check a recorded native guest configuration. Physical hardware tests apply to the exact tested devices; human review covers its stated scope. These levels are distinct. A simulation, green CI job or second AI review cannot substitute for a required native, hardware or human check. Is it ready for daily use? No. There is no supported production release. The full native test release, physical hardware acceptance and maintenance path remain open. Read the QEMU exploration route if a bounded development build is useful. Acceptance rules ↗ Current project state ↗ Test strategy ↗ Verification records ↗"},{"title":"Frequently asked questions","route":"/docs/faq/","group":"Decide if it fits","summary":"Short answers about Linux, installation, readiness, privacy, licensing and support.","text":"Is Baryon OS Linux? No. The native system does not use the Linux, BSD, XNU, Windows or Android kernel as its foundation. Linux may be used as a development host, and public standards, firmware interfaces, emulators and reviewed libraries may be used without changing the guest kernel's identity. Can it be installed on a personal computer? No supported installer or hardware profile is available. The documented route is an isolated QEMU virtual machine with disposable images. Do not attach a host disk or treat a development image as a daily-use system. What works today? Selected boot, kernel, process, capability, storage, networking, UI, SDK and research paths have implementation and evidence. Integration varies. Use Status and the source-bound records rather than inferring readiness from folders, screenshots or test totals. Does local-first mean offline only? No. Normal internet access and continuity are requirements. Local-first means that local work remains possible and remote storage or synchronization is explicit, bounded and not a silent vendor fallback. Will existing Windows, macOS or Linux apps run? No general compatibility promise exists. Baryon OS has its own SDK and application model. Controlled ports and maintained engines may be investigated, but format support, permissions, performance and failure behavior require separate acceptance. Is the project proven secure or quantum-proof? No. The project defines security requirements and investigates post-quantum protection, but claims remain limited to their recorded tests and review scope. A quantum simulator is not a physical QPU or proof of speedup. What license and support apply? Project-owned software in the file register uses MPL-2.0; other material retains its applicable terms. There is no production support commitment. Security reports follow the private route in the repository security policy. About the project → Glossary → Licensing scope ↗"},{"title":"Desktop and Material","route":"/docs/design/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"How Orbit, familiar desktop interactions and the document model fit together.","text":"What should the desktop feel like? The intended desktop keeps familiar actions: start an app, search, arrange windows, find a file and change settings. Orbit is the planned dock, with the Zenit launcher and Signaal search. The Orbit-inspired artwork on this website conveys the project's visual direction; it is not a screenshot of a finished OS. What is Materiaal? Materiaal is the model for documents, collections, history and access rights. An app is a tool for a permitted task, rather than the owner of every file it can reach. The intended everyday path is to find a document, grant one app limited access, save a revision, and later reopen it with another tool. This model belongs in a familiar Files app and permission dialogs, not in a mandatory unfamiliar home screen. What makes the design usable and accessible? Keyboard operation, readable scaling, clear focus, understandable permission language and reduced motion are design requirements. The native desktop, full permission enforcement and independent usability results still require further integration and testing. See progress and evidence before interpreting a design or prototype as a working feature. Desktop shell design ↗ Materiaal product specification ↗ Design and task criteria ↗ Materiaal reading guide ↗"},{"title":"Profiles and accessibility","route":"/docs/profiles/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"The intended first start, local accounts, trusted sign-in and adaptive experience.","text":"What should first start look like? The design calls for a recognisable local setup: language, keyboard, optional network, a local account, recovery material, optional PIN, storage choice and a short tour. A vendor account is not mandatory. An interrupted setup must not leave a half-created identity or keys. How are local users separated? The intended model distinguishes administrator, standard and temporary guest roles. Each user needs a separate namespace and keys. An administrator may manage accounts and reclaim local space but must not gain a function that decrypts another user's content. Password and administrator secrets belong only in trusted system surfaces. How does accessibility fit the design? Accessibility is part of the product identity rather than a separate theme. Keyboard use, readable scaling, contrast, reduced motion, assistive technology and alternative input are baseline requirements. Additional visual, motion, typography and stimulus preferences should combine without turning off essential safety or information. What is implemented? Native session and UI components exist, but the complete first-start, authentication, multi-user key separation and native accessibility acceptance remain open. Browser prototypes can test interaction ideas; they do not demonstrate kernel enforcement or assistive behavior in the native OS. Profiles and users design ↗ Design and accessibility ambition ↗ Desktop and Material →"},{"title":"Apps and permissions","route":"/docs/apps/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"How apps, packages, data access and replaceable tools are meant to remain separate.","text":"What does installing an app authorize? Installation should authorize no general access by itself. Package provenance, permission to install, permission to launch, access to selected material, network destinations and administrative actions are separate decisions. A signed package can establish origin and integrity under a selected key; it cannot establish that the app is safe. How should an app reach personal data? The intended route uses explicit task grants. A document picker or other trusted portal grants a bounded handle for the selected object and operation. Helpers and extensions must not inherit broader rights or bypass revocation. Removing an app is not permission to erase user documents. Where can apps come from? The package design allows multiple explicitly chosen sources. Source rotation or revocation can stop new verification without silently deleting already installed apps. A production installer still needs durable source authority, native storage integration, transactional recovery, user-facing consent and a complete launch boundary. What application ecosystem exists now? The repository contains an SDK, example applications and package-verification and app-management cores. A publicly documented SDK criterion is accepted. Native installation, update/removal, broad everyday application coverage and integrated per-task permissions remain open. Package verification and app management ↗ Application SDK ↗ Packages and updates →"},{"title":"Storage, sync and NAS","route":"/docs/storage/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"Local work, encrypted remote copies, personal NAS roles, conflicts and recovery.","text":"Where should everyday work live? Core tasks are intended to work on local storage. Documents, collections, metadata and revisions belong to the Material model. Confirmed changes need explicit durability and recovery behavior; an uncertain write must not be reported as safely stored. How can a personal or shared NAS be used? A user may explicitly pair a personal NAS or receive capacity from family or friends. The client encrypts content before transfer. The NAS administrator can set quotas, revoke access and remove hosted copies, but should receive no content keys or permission to erase the user's local copies. Can an external storage provider be selected? Yes, as an optional opaque storage layer. The provider receives encrypted objects and limited metadata needed for storage, not Baryon OS content keys or automatic access to other data. It is not a silent fallback or a source of key escrow. Availability, rollback and metadata leakage remain part of the threat model. How should synchronization behave? Previously authorized devices should resume within the selected scope, prefer an available local route when appropriate and preserve offline work. Concurrent changes, interrupted transfers, quota exhaustion, revocation and identity changes require explicit conflict or recovery outcomes. Native multi-device integration remains open. NAS and internet design ↗ Encrypted object storage ↗ Privacy and data control →"},{"title":"Privacy and data control","route":"/docs/privacy/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"Where data may go, which choices remain explicit and what local-first actually means.","text":"Does local-first mean offline? No. Core tasks are intended to work locally, while internet access remains a product requirement. Network permission is separate from permission to read a document or reach a NAS. A remote service cannot become a silent fallback for personal storage. Who controls shared storage? The design allows explicitly paired personal or family NAS storage and an explicitly chosen external provider for client-side encrypted objects. The NAS administrator may manage space and remove hosted copies, but receives no content key or right to erase local copies. Encryption does not guarantee availability or hide all size, timing and connection metadata. Native pairing, synchronisation and provider integration remain open work. What can an app or assistant access? Installing an app must not grant general file, device or network access. Rights should be limited to the resource, operation and duration needed for the task. System AI inference is required to stay on the user's device without network permission or cloud fallback; a useful native AI assistant has not yet met acceptance. See research tracks for the evidence boundary. Can a privacy preference turn off core protection? No. The Security Floor requires process isolation, capability enforcement, key isolation, memory protection and authentic packages and updates regardless of privacy preferences. This is a binding design requirement, not a claim that the whole OS has been proven secure. The progress chapter links to accepted evidence and remaining work. Binding privacy contract ↗ NAS and internet design ↗ Acceptance and evidence ↗ Local-first reading guide ↗"},{"title":"Local AI","route":"/docs/local-ai/","group":"Experience and personal data","summary":"The on-device assistant direction, model boundaries and current experiment limits.","text":"What is local AI meant to do? The product direction treats AI as an optional tool for explicitly selected work, such as clarifying, summarizing, comparing or translating material. Normal editing must remain useful without AI. A model output does not create a permission or authorize a system action. Can inference use the cloud? No. System AI inference is required to run on the user's device without inference network permission, NAS fallback or silent provider request. Model download is a separate, explicitly authorized process that must not receive document context, history, indexes or content keys. What is the first experiment? The current proposal evaluates a pinned small model and a Rust engine candidate under bounded memory, context, cancellation and numeric requirements. The first native tasks are deliberately narrow. Model provenance, tokenizer/configuration binding, answer quality, latency and resource behavior still require measurement. What has not been established? A parser or host model experiment is not a useful native assistant. The roadmap still requires real local inference, bounded selected context, denial of upload routes, graceful insufficient-resource behavior and an OS that remains responsive. No general quality, language or hardware-acceleration claim follows from the proposal. First local AI experiment ↗ AI privacy contract ↗ Research tracks →"},{"title":"System architecture","route":"/docs/architecture/","group":"Inside the system","summary":"Follow the boot chain from firmware and the Rust kernel through services to apps.","text":"How do the pieces fit together? The first development target is x86_64 in a QEMU virtual machine with UEFI firmware. This simplified path helps explain the layers; it does not mean that every planned service or app is integrated. UEFI firmware OVMF provides the boot interface inside the virtual machine. Project loader The Baryon OS loader prepares the kernel and passes boot information to it. Kernel The independently developed Rust kernel manages privileged resources and process boundaries. Processes and services Isolated components perform bounded jobs through defined interfaces. SDK, apps and UI Application-facing interfaces and the desktop sit above those boundaries; their integration remains in progress. What makes the kernel independent? The native system does not use Linux, BSD, XNU or another existing OS kernel as its foundation. Development tools, UEFI firmware, public standards and reviewed libraries can still be used. A Linux development computer does not turn the guest OS into a Linux distribution. How are permissions meant to cross layers? A capability is a kernel-checked right to a particular resource and operation. The design calls for bounded requests between processes: selecting one document for an app should not grant access to every file. The privacy and security chapter explains the intended limits; the progress chapter separates implemented paths from full release acceptance. Project overview ↗ Kernel and security contract ↗ Current integration state ↗ Architecture reading guide ↗"},{"title":"Security model","route":"/docs/security/","group":"Inside the system","summary":"Capabilities, isolation, the Security Floor, threat boundaries and evidence limits.","text":"What is a capability? A capability is bounded authority over a resource and operation. The design uses kernel-validated handles rather than trusting an arbitrary name or pointer. IPC carries bounded requests between isolated processes; the receiving service must still validate inputs and delegated rights. What is the Security Floor? Privacy preferences may change explanations or selected sharing, but must not disable process isolation, capability enforcement, key isolation, memory protection, package/update authenticity or protection of unrelated data. Installing an app or selecting remote storage does not create broad implicit authority. What threats are in scope? Contracts cover malicious or faulty apps, invalid IPC, resource exhaustion, storage failure, package tampering, network denial and selected key and recovery paths. Other boundaries require their own evidence: a compromised kernel or trusted service, physical attacks, firmware, supply chain, rollback, side channels and unsupported hardware cannot be dismissed by one subsystem test. Is the OS proven secure? No. Accepted criteria establish only their recorded configurations and cases. The complete release still requires broader native integration, physical evidence and targeted independent expert review. Security problems should be reported privately rather than disclosed in public project channels. Kernel and security contract ↗ Security policy ↗ Private vulnerability report ↗"},{"title":"Packages and updates","route":"/docs/packages/","group":"Inside the system","summary":"Provenance, installation, permissions, system updates and remaining integration work.","text":"How is package provenance checked? The current verification core binds a versioned manifest to an application image and checks its length, digest, ABI range and two signatures against keys from an explicitly selected source. The package cannot name its own trusted key. Verification establishes integrity and provenance under that policy, not safety or permission. What happens during installation? The intended installer reserves space, streams the verified immutable image, commits it transactionally and updates a durable catalogue. Interruption and uncertain I/O must fail closed and recover from authoritative state. Installation grants no document, network, device or administrative capability. How do app updates differ from system updates? App updates follow selected publisher/source policy and preserve the separation between code and user data. System updates additionally need an authentic boot and distribution chain, safe migration, interruption recovery, downgrade resistance and a valid previous system slot. Both paths require explicit provenance and recovery behavior. What works now? Package verification, source policy and host-model transaction/catalogue work exist, while the native installer and full app lifecycle remain open. Signed system update and rollback behavior has accepted evidence for its recorded criterion, but the broader trusted release and physical recovery chain is not complete. App package specification ↗ Signed update design ↗ Apps and permissions →"},{"title":"Research tracks","route":"/docs/research/","group":"Inside the system","summary":"Explore work on local AI, post-quantum protection and quantum execution.","text":"What is the central research question? Baryon OS investigates whether an independently developed kernel, services and product experience can form a useful operating system while preserving clear authority over data. Design proposals, host experiments, native QEMU results and physical hardware tests answer different questions; none can stand in for a supported daily-use release. What is the local AI goal? System AI is required to run on the user's device, with bounded access to selected data and no inference network permission or silent cloud fallback. Host-side model and numerical foundations exist, but a useful native inference task and its network-denial acceptance remain open. What does quantum research include? The work separates post-quantum transport, local quantum computation and possible hybrid hardware. A native QEMU software simulator and job interface have bounded acceptance evidence for reference circuits, quotas and cancellation. This does not demonstrate a physical quantum processing unit, quantum speedup or general hardware support. Post-quantum transport and an OS control layer for hybrid hardware remain separate research and integration work. Current research criteria ↗ Native simulator acceptance ↗ Quantum research scope ↗ Research tracks reading guide ↗"},{"title":"Try the development build","route":"/docs/try/","group":"Try and verify","summary":"How to start a bounded QEMU experiment without treating it as an installer.","text":"Can Baryon OS be installed on a personal device? No supported installation on a physical PC or phone is available. The documented route is an isolated QEMU virtual machine for development and testing. It is not a daily-use release or an installer. How can a development build be started? First follow the current development guide for the pinned toolchain, firmware and host setup. From a repository checkout, run the basic checks in order: python3 -B tools/preflight.py --strict python3 -B tools/build.py python3 -B tools/run.py --smoke Use only disposable virtual images, never a real host disk. The selected development profile and its documented output determine whether the smoke test passed. Preflight checks tools; compilation checks a build; neither alone proves runtime behaviour. What does the smoke test show? A passing smoke run establishes a bounded native path in the recorded QEMU configuration. It does not establish a complete desktop, supported hardware, safe personal-data use or release acceptance. Consult roadmap and evidence before interpreting a result. Development setup ↗ What tests demonstrate ↗ Release acceptance ↗"},{"title":"Hardware and release readiness","route":"/docs/hardware/","group":"Try and verify","summary":"Why virtual-machine progress differs from PC support and a maintained release.","text":"Which environment is the development baseline? The first target is x86_64 with UEFI firmware in QEMU. A fixed virtual configuration makes native behavior reproducible while the kernel, services and product are integrated. It does not establish support for an arbitrary laptop, desktop, GPU, Wi-Fi adapter or power controller. Why is physical installation not recommended? The release requirements still include a supported hardware matrix, safe installation and recovery, graphics and audio usability, network hardware, sleep/wake, thermal and battery behavior, DMA/IOMMU boundaries and repeated combined workloads. Those functions can fail in ways a virtual machine does not model. What would a supported device need? A named reference configuration needs its exact firmware and components recorded, its normal and failure paths tested, and its limitations published. Evidence for one device does not imply support for every PC. Real-disk installation or firmware operations require separate explicit authorization and recoverable procedures. What is the release boundary? A release is more than a bootable image. It requires reproducible artifacts, licenses and provenance, accessible recovery, patch and maintenance arrangements, responsible reporting, sustained workload evidence and completed product tasks on the final integrated source. Full release requirements ↗ Safe QEMU route → Current readiness →"},{"title":"Testing and evidence","route":"/docs/testing/","group":"Try and verify","summary":"What host, build, QEMU, physical-hardware and human evidence can establish.","text":"Why are evidence levels separate? Every result answers a bounded question for a source version, configuration and environment. Combining unlike results into one vague “tested” label would overstate what is known. Host test Checks selected code or a prototype on the development OS; it does not establish execution under the Baryon OS kernel. Build check Shows that selected sources compile; it does not establish runtime behavior. Native QEMU Checks guest behavior in a recorded virtual profile; it does not establish physical-device support. Physical or human Applies to the named hardware or review/task scope and cannot be generalized silently. What makes evidence traceable? A useful record names the source commit, commands, exit status, profile, expected markers, logs and limitations. Native smoke acceptance requires the documented guest markers and exit behavior, not simply a process that ended without visible errors. How does evidence affect the roadmap? A fixed criterion earns points only after every clause and required evidence level is accepted. Partial implementation, more test cases or a second AI review does not award partial points. A regression can reopen a criterion. What does review not prove? Automated review can identify defects; it is not an independent human security or accessibility audit. A simulation can inform interaction design but cannot prove native enforcement. Missing evidence remains open rather than being inferred from a similar component. Test strategy ↗ Verification records ↗ Roadmap and evidence →"},{"title":"Development workflow","route":"/docs/workflow/","group":"Explore the project","summary":"How a bounded change moves from current state and contracts to evidence and review.","text":"Where does a development task start? Start from the current branch, source version, working-tree state and project checkpoint. Read the contract and local instructions for the files in scope. Historical designs and old research assignments are context, not automatic permission to replace current code. How is a task bounded? One task records a goal, invariant, exact write set, acceptance commands and evidence limit. Ownership must be clear before editing. The smallest complete change reuses existing interfaces and avoids unrelated cleanup, policy changes or weakened validation. How is a result checked? Run targeted tests first, then the required project gates. Preserve complete logs and exact exit codes. Review the full diff, fix root causes and keep tests, security, accessibility and failure handling intact. Anything not run stays explicitly marked as not run. How does work reach the project? A coherent verified step is committed, pushed and presented as a draft pull request. Hosted checks apply to the current head. Merge or auto-merge requires an explicit project-owner decision; a pull request or green build does not itself accept a roadmap criterion. Contribution guide ↗ Verification workflow ↗ Contribution routes →"},{"title":"Repository guide","route":"/docs/repository/","group":"Explore the project","summary":"A map of the loader, kernel, services, drivers, SDK, UI, tests and research areas.","text":"Where is the native system? boot/ UEFI loader and the handoff into the project kernel. kernel/ and crates/ Privileged implementation and reusable no-std contracts. drivers/ and services/ Device paths and bounded system services; some host prototypes are separately labelled. sdk/ and userspace/ Application-facing interfaces, examples and ring-3 probes. ui/ Native UI, the design system and separately labelled browser prototypes. Where are tests and research? tests/ and tools/ contain host tests, build/run harnesses and evidence tooling. experiments/ holds AI and quantum research. Folder presence alone does not establish native integration. Where are decisions and evidence? docs/ contains current contracts, implementation specifications, decisions, project state, progress and source-bound verification. Begin with the README and current project state before following a subsystem document. Architecture map ↗ Open the repository ↗ Development workflow →"},{"title":"Sources and documentation","route":"/docs/sources/","group":"Explore the project","summary":"Which project source answers which question and how the website and wiki relate.","text":"Which source is authoritative? The versioned repository is authoritative for current product contracts, implementation, progress and evidence. The README introduces the project; PROJECT-STATE records the current checkpoint; PROGRESS defines formal acceptance; FULL-TEST-RELEASE preserves the binding product matrix. What is this website for? This website is a visitor-oriented reading layer. It groups common questions and links to the relevant source. It deliberately avoids copying full technical specifications or maintaining a second progress register. If a website summary and a newer repository contract differ, the repository contract prevails. What role does the GitHub wiki have? The wiki provides a navigable English introduction and inspired this Docs topic map. It identifies its own source baseline and is not automatically synchronized with every repository change. Current status, commands and acceptance must therefore be checked in the repository. How can a claim be verified? Follow the link to the contract or evidence, check its source version and distinguish design, implementation and executed verification. External standards and research can support a design choice but do not establish that Baryon OS implemented or accepted it. GitHub wiki ↗ Wiki publication guide ↗ Current project state ↗ Formal progress ↗"},{"title":"Glossary","route":"/docs/glossary/","group":"Explore the project","summary":"Look up terms such as kernel, capability, NAS and QEMU in plain English.","text":"Terms used in this guide These definitions are reading aids. The linked project contracts give each term its precise technical meaning. Kernel The core of the operating system. It manages memory, processor time and access to shared resources. Capability A kernel-checked right to act on a particular resource. IPC Interprocess communication: messages or requests crossing process boundaries. Ring 3 The x86 user privilege level for isolated processes outside the kernel. UEFI The firmware interface used by the project's loader. OVMF UEFI firmware used for the project's QEMU virtual machines. QEMU The emulator and virtual-machine environment for scoped guest tests. Native Running under the Baryon OS kernel, rather than on the development host. Host prototype A test or implementation running on the development computer's OS; it does not establish native integration. NAS Network-attached storage. Hosting encrypted data does not automatically grant the administrator a content key. Security Floor Core protections that a privacy preference must not disable. ISA Instruction-set architecture: the processor instruction model targeted by compiled code. QPU Quantum processing unit. Software simulator results are a different evidence category. For how these terms fit together, start with architecture , then read privacy and security and progress and evidence . Repository glossary ↗ Kernel terminology ↗ Test evidence levels ↗"}];
  const form = document.querySelector(".site-search");
  const input = document.querySelector("#site-search-input");
  const status = document.querySelector("#search-result-status");
  const list = document.querySelector("#search-result-list");

  if (!form || !input || !status || !list) {
    return;
  }

  const normalize = (value) => value
    .normalize("NFKD")
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, "")
    .toLocaleLowerCase("en")
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9%]+/g, " ")
    .trim();

  const searchable = searchIndex.map((entry) => ({
    ...entry,
    normalizedTitle: normalize(entry.title),
    normalizedSummary: normalize(entry.summary),
    normalizedText: normalize(entry.text),
  }));

  const makeResult = (entry) => {
    const item = document.createElement("li");
    const link = document.createElement("a");
    const group = document.createElement("span");
    const title = document.createElement("strong");
    const summary = document.createElement("span");

    link.className = "search-result-card";
    link.href = entry.route;
    group.className = "search-result-group";
    group.textContent = entry.group;
    title.textContent = entry.title;
    summary.textContent = entry.summary;
    link.append(group, title, summary);
    item.append(link);
    return item;
  };

  const score = (entry, query, terms) => {
    const combined = `${entry.normalizedTitle} ${entry.normalizedSummary} ${entry.normalizedText}`;
    if (!terms.every((term) => combined.includes(term))) {
      return -1;
    }
    let value = 0;
    if (entry.normalizedTitle.includes(query)) value += 20;
    if (entry.normalizedSummary.includes(query)) value += 8;
    if (entry.normalizedText.includes(query)) value += 2;
    for (const term of terms) {
      if (entry.normalizedTitle.includes(term)) value += 6;
      if (entry.normalizedSummary.includes(term)) value += 3;
      if (entry.normalizedText.includes(term)) value += 1;
    }
    return value;
  };

  const render = (rawQuery, updateAddress = true) => {
    const query = normalize(rawQuery);
    list.replaceChildren();

    if (updateAddress) {
      const url = new URL(window.location.href);
      if (rawQuery.trim()) url.searchParams.set("q", rawQuery.trim());
      else url.searchParams.delete("q");
      window.history.replaceState(null, "", url);
    }

    if (!query) {
      status.textContent = "Enter a word or phrase to search the visitor guides.";
      return;
    }

    const terms = query.split(/\s+/);
    const matches = searchable
      .map((entry) => ({ entry, value: score(entry, query, terms) }))
      .filter((match) => match.value >= 0)
      .sort((left, right) => right.value - left.value || left.entry.title.localeCompare(right.entry.title))
      .slice(0, 12);

    if (!matches.length) {
      status.textContent = `No pages found for “${rawQuery.trim()}”. Try a broader term or open all Docs topics.`;
      return;
    }

    status.textContent = `${matches.length} ${matches.length === 1 ? "page" : "pages"} found for “${rawQuery.trim()}”.`;
    list.append(...matches.map((match) => makeResult(match.entry)));
  };

  form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    render(input.value);
  });

  input.addEventListener("input", () => render(input.value));

  document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
    const target = event.target;
    const editing = target instanceof HTMLElement
      && (target.matches("input, textarea, select") || target.isContentEditable);
    if (event.key === "/" && !editing && !event.metaKey && !event.ctrlKey && !event.altKey) {
      event.preventDefault();
      input.focus();
    }
  });

  const initialQuery = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get("q") || "";
  input.value = initialQuery;
  render(initialQuery, false);
})();
